okay let's get to audience questions  first one hey David could you lay out a  basic financial plan for someone who  works and is middle-class but just has  no idea what to do  I understand lots of people aren't in a  position to save any money but it would  be great to hear you talk about what  middle-class people should be doing yeah  I I've hesitated to do this number one  because I don't want to be condescending  to anybody based on their financial  situation and also because I don't want  to be giving financial advice so let me  say none of this this is all  hypothetical it's not advice for  anyone's specific situation but some  basic principles are if you don't have a  thousand dollars in an emergency fund  you need a thousand dollars in an  emergency fund and not having it I would  consider an emergency you need to  significantly curtail all spending until  you have that thousand dollars that we  did a story not long ago about how some  very high percentage of Americans would  not be able to afford an unexpected four  hundred dollar expense without like  putting it on a credit card or borrowing  from friends that's a real problem so  you need a thousand dollars because of  the health care system we have you  probably in addition to the thousand  should also have whatever your  deductible is if your deductible yearly  is two thousand dollars on health  insurance you probably want that in  addition to the thousand-dollar  emergency fund that's just a reality and  then you want to be working towards a  three to six month not now it's no  longer an emergency fund but sort of an  income replacement fund the ideal  scenario would be to have six months of  if you if your income went to zero you  could pay for everything you're  currently paying for for six months that  would be a great place to be and whether  that's practical for different people  really depends on their situation you  want to avoid high interest credit card  debt it you ideally would just be paying  I use my credit card like a debit card I  use it for frequent flier miles and I  pay it off every month not everybody can  do that I understand that but you want  to be avoiding any fees and interest  that are not necessary because it just  takes away it's money you're just giving  away to financial institutions and then  you do want to think a little bit about  your sort of general asset allocation  and this  includes thinking about retirement plans  what how much cash should you have  versus money in tax deferred investment  accounts versus in real estate for  example so again everybody's situation  is very different  just having cash you know having a whole  bunch of cash and then not really  contributing to anything that is going  to grow in terms of a retirement account  or an investment account is not  particularly wise I'm being deliberately  vague again because I am NOT giving  specific financial advice but as an  example I used to invest in mutual funds  in real estate investment trusts are  EITS  REITs as they're called and it's like a  proxy for investing in real estate when  I actually started getting involved in  in real estate directly with properties  that I'm involved with I realized it's  too much real estate I should stop  investing in the REITs and maybe look at  reallocating that to something else my  philosophy on investing is don't pick  stocks look for the lowest cost broadest  mutual funds and figure out what is your  balance between stocks and bonds there  are lots of tools out there one says you  take the number 120 and subtract the  number 100 and subtract your age and  that's the percent you want to be in  stock so if you're in if you're 30 years  old you want to be 70% in stocks which  actually seems a little bit conservative  maybe for my tastes but there's all  these tools that will help you figure  that out when it comes to things like  mortgages very often it is not logical  to take a huge chunk of money and pay  off the mortgage if you actually figure  out the numbers financially because  there is a tax benefit to paying  mortgage interest and if your mortgage  is 4% and you can invest in something  that historically returns 7% you're  better off taking the money and putting  it into the investment instead of into  the mortgage but everybody's situation  is different there's a sort of  psychological value to paying down or  paying off a mortgage which what might  make it the right thing for some  people even if they are effectively  accepting a lower return as a result so  these it's really hard to have a sort of  overarching financial plan but my my  sort of tips in general are you need a  thousand dollars right away plus your  health insurance exposed exposure the  amount you're exposed to health  insurance spending in an emergency fund  working towards six months of expenses  in case you earn nothing for six months  you want to ensure that you are not on  the hedonic treadmill and that your  expenses are properly attuned to your  income and you don't want to start  spending more every time you make a  little more don't get into the cycle of  living beyond your means in terms of  using high interest credit card debt for  anything other than absolute necessities  again if you use it like a debit card to  accrue points and you pay it off and  fold that's okay  and then you want to be contributing as  much as you can in the most advantaged  way possible towards retirement so if  your employer offers a 50% match up to  six percent of your income you if you  can afford it want to be contributing 6  percent of your income to that  retirement plan to get the 3 percent  from your employer because that's quote  free money of course nothing is really  free you can make the case that your  salary is lower because your employer is  already accounting for that in the offer  to match your contributions 50 cents on  the dollar up to 6 percent but still  it's money that's available and you want  to be taking advantage of that and and  really a lot of this stuff comes to do  comes back to the tax impact so it is  important to read up on or have an  accountant that can advise you for  example about with the recent from tax  reform now that there has been a limit  placed on the deductibility of certain  types of taxes is it as advantageous to  have that mortgage or would it be better  to look at paying it down and investing  the money elsewhere etc this is all very  vague stuff but I hope I'm at least  telling you sort of like a basic  framework that I apply and again when it  comes to any kind of investment one of  your top priorities should be  reduce the cost Vanguard and betterment  without giving any particular  endorsements  have fees that are so low sometimes  point oh three point oh five percent  compared to what sometimes our one or  two percent management fees for similar  asset allocation in other institutions  so pay very very close attention to that  okay that does it for that question  let's move on and I'm glad to take more  questions about this someone actually  had the idea David you should do on your  twitch stream when it whenever you  launch it you should just do like  personal finance stuff since your  background is in personal finance from  grad school it's an interesting idea I  think I'm just kind of out of the game  and I wouldn't really be the right  person to be giving that kind of advice  which could be funny in a sense but I  also don't want to damage anybody's  financial circumstances
 

